  "rules": {
    "seller_claim_not_independent_outcome_v1": "The four frozen shopping pages establish exact seller-authored offer and marketing statements, not the truth of a wellness outcome. The Lakanto page says detox and destress, high in antioxidants, probiotics, and L-theanine, and claims metabolism and endurance help. The Tealyra page says blood cleanser, detox, diuretic, liver toxins, and kidney filtering. The Lifeboost page says low acid, pH 6 or higher, and gentle on stomach and teeth. The La Republica page says seven mushrooms provide focus, clean energy, immunity, antioxidant, liver, stomach, inflammation, and no-jitters benefits. Prices, ratings, review counts, organic or testing language, ingredient names, and return promises do not independently establish those outcomes, serving dose, assay result, clinical comparator, incidence, or applicability to this drinker.",
    "named_detox_endpoint_boundary_v1": "Alternative-medicine detoxification is described as removal of unspecified toxins, distinct from the liver and kidneys' ordinary physiological filtering. The captured concept page states that toxins generally remain undefined and that there is little to no evidence of toxic accumulation or supporting factual evidence for detox diets and commercial claims. A testable claim therefore needs a named substance, plausible exposure, validated before-and-after measurement, relevant clinical endpoint, comparison, and safety evidence. Without those fields, detox, blood cleansing, kidney filtering, liver toxin elimination, metabolism, and endurance copy remains marketing language rather than a verified benefit.",
    "antioxidant_activity_not_health_outcome_v1": "An antioxidant can inhibit oxidation, and compounds may show antioxidant properties in vitro, but the concept page says there may be little evidence for in-vivo antioxidant properties and that dietary supplements marketed as antioxidants have not been shown to maintain health or prevent disease in humans. Ingredient presence or an antioxidant assay therefore cannot be promoted into a product-specific clinical benefit without an independently quantified compound and dose, bioavailability, relevant population, comparator, endpoint, and replicated outcome. Matcha or mushroom antioxidant language may describe an ingredient or laboratory property while leaving a health claim unresolved.",
    "caffeine_dose_timing_and_person_boundary_v1": "The caffeine page says effects are dose-dependent, may improve alertness, can disrupt sleep or cause anxiety for some people while others show little disturbance, and lists anxiety, jitteriness, insomnia, and increased sleep latency among common minor undesired effects. It also gives an adult elimination half-life range of three to seven hours and notes tolerance. The community pages show individual questions and widely differing experiences, not controlled dose evidence. Evaluate energy, focus, crash, and no-jitters claims only after measuring caffeine and other active ingredients per prepared serving, timing, frequency, tolerance, sleep window, co-exposures, and the drinker's repeatable response.",
    "low_acid_measurement_not_symptom_guarantee_v1": "The low-acid concept page defines low-acid coffee using a critical pH above 5.5 or a relative acid reduction, explains that brew factors affect final pH, and reports that most tested products labeled low acid remained within the expected range for regular coffee. The Lifeboost page's pH 6-or-higher and stomach-or-teeth language is seller copy. Verify the exact lot and prepared recipe with an independent pH method and disclose brew conditions. Even a verified higher pH does not by itself guarantee relief of reflux, gastritis, ulcer, nausea, dental erosion, or any other person's symptoms; caffeine, dose, timing, milk, food, and individual condition remain separate variables.",
    "fungal_drug_history_not_mushroom_coffee_efficacy_v1": "The medicinal-fungi page distinguishes fungal metabolites successfully developed into prescription drugs from traditional species and mushroom dietary supplements. It says mushroom supplements marketed for health benefits lack sufficient scientific evidence for safety or effectiveness and can vary because of inconsistent processing and labeling. Naming lion's mane, reishi, chaga, cordyceps, shiitake, maitake, and turkey tail therefore does not establish the exact coffee's extract identity, dose, purity, bioavailability, interaction profile, focus, memory, immunity, liver, stomach, inflammation, antioxidant, crash, or jitter outcome. Those claims require product-specific quality and outcome evidence.",
    "community_anecdote_and_question_scope_v1": "One black-tea author reports anxiety after a long steep and self-identifies as a slow caffeine metabolizer; one infrequent coffee drinker reports overnight wakefulness after a venti Americano; one author asks what creates an energy-drink kick; and one asks why black and green tea caffeine might differ. These pages identify variables worth checking, including serving size, preparation, dose, timing, tolerance, and individual response. They do not establish prevalence, diagnose metabolism, verify product composition, compare the four exact products, or prove any detox, antioxidant, low-acid, mushroom, focus, or no-jitters claim.",
    "evidence_bounded_wellness_claim_decision_v1": "For each exact product, record the literal claim, seller status, ingredient and prepared-serving dose, independent lot-specific measurement, target population, comparator, validated endpoint, effect size, harms, interactions, and uncertainty. Classify unmeasured seller copy as label-only; classify a generic chemical or physiological mechanism as mechanism-supported but outcome-unresolved; and treat a claim as decision-relevant only when product-specific measurement and applicable outcome evidence clear predeclared thresholds. For ordinary taste, caffeine, price, and tolerability preferences, compare one variable at a time under a reversible trial after safety checks. Do not pay a wellness premium for unresolved claims: choose the cheapest acceptable claim-neutral beverage, keep shopping, or defer. Persistent or severe symptoms require qualified medical evaluation rather than beverage self-treatment."
